          "id": "mol-u1-s2",
          "title": "RNA molecular classes",
          "content": "<h3>Mechanistic foundation</h3>\n<p>RNA uses ribose and uracil and is typically single-stranded, yet base pairing produces stems, loops and complex folded architectures. Messenger RNA carries coding information; ribosomal and transfer RNAs participate directly in translation; small nuclear RNAs help splice pre-mRNA; microRNAs and long noncoding RNAs can regulate processes in particular contexts. RNA stability depends on sequence, structure, associated proteins and compartment. Some RNAs act as enzymes; the ribosome peptidyl-transferase center is an important example of RNA-mediated catalysis.</p>\n<h3>Stepwise reasoning from molecular events to an observation</h3>\n<p>A useful molecular explanation begins by identifying the actual substrate and product in <strong>rna molecular classes</strong>, rather than merely naming a molecule associated with the phenomenon. The starting claim is that rNA uses ribose and uracil and is typically single-stranded, yet base pairing produces stems, loops and complex folded architectures. In an active cell, production and consumption often occur simultaneously. For rna molecular classes, the distinction is especially consequential because some RNAs act as enzymes; the ribosome peptidyl-transferase center is an important example of RNA-mediated catalysis.</p>\n<h3>Study design: choosing what to observe</h3>\n<p>Separate total RNA into size classes and use an appropriate hybridization probe to follow one transcript after transcriptional shutoff. Measure both the RNA itself and a candidate protein product, since a change in RNA abundance is not necessarily a matching change in protein.</p>\n<p>For <strong>rna molecular classes</strong>, write down the expected results for three matched conditions: an unperturbed sample, a targeted perturbation of the proposed molecular step, and a rescue that restores function without otherwise changing the assay. A fourth condition should deliberately test an alternative explanation.</p>\n<h3>Quantitative thinking without false precision</h3>\n<p>For rna molecular classes, distinguishing the number of molecules from the proportion of molecules in a state is often essential.</p>\n<p>Start analysis with the limiting cases of an idealized model. When writing about rna molecular classes, state where idealization ends and empirical validation must begin.</p>\n<h3>Discriminating causes and ruling out artifacts</h3>\n<p>Not every transcript encodes a protein. Detection of a noncoding RNA does not demonstrate a particular regulatory function without perturbation and rescue. Build two alternative hypotheses that could mimic the predicted observation.</p>\n<p>A meaningful negative result needs equal scrutiny. Establish the dynamic range with positive and negative controls.</p>\n<h3>Specific molecular investigation</h3>\n<p>Consider a tissue sample yielding a prominent RNA band near 2 kilobases. The band could contain many different transcripts with similar size, including coding messages and long noncoding species. If a lab labels newly synthesized RNA and then measures total signal at one endpoint, it cannot distinguish production from turnover for any particular RNA class. A targeted probe to a defined exon, a probe for an intronic precursor, and an independently quantified mature RNA would give contrasting evidence about synthesis versus processing. Ribosomal RNA often dominates total-RNA mass, so normalizing an individual messenger RNA to all recovered RNA has context-specific assumptions. When one knockdown alters translation but scarcely changes transcript abundance, an RNA-dependent translational mechanism becomes plausible, but remains to be verified by a protein-production assay.</p>\n",
          "secNumber": "1.2",
