          "secNumber": "1.5",
          "heading": "Unit overview",
          "content": "Information stored in nucleic acids must be represented at multiple levels: polymer chemistry, sequence, transcriptional units and observable phenotype. The same base pair can be a stable part of a DNA helix and a mutable target of damage, while the same DNA locus may support several RNA products. The organizing question is which information is copied, which is interpreted, and which is regulated. Distinguish the chemical substrate from an abstract sequence string. A reader should be able to describe strand polarity without confusing it with the orientation of the printed reference genome. Throughout the unit, use separate symbols for gene dosage, transcript abundance and protein output.",
          "simulations": []
