          "secNumber": "3.5",
          "heading": "Unit overview",
          "content": "Genomes are chemically vulnerable but actively maintained. The term repair must always be connected to a substrate, a molecular pathway and an outcome. Removing a damaged base is not identical to restoring sequence, and sealing a break is not always error free. Population survival is a poor substitute for lesion-specific recovery if severely damaged cells disappear from the assay. This unit compares multiple ways of handling damage and explores how recombination and mobile elements also contribute to genome variability. Readers should distinguish conservative restoration from sequence-changing outcomes.",
