          "id": "mol-u4-s1",
          "title": "Promoters and initiation",
          "content": "<h3>Mechanistic foundation</h3>\n<p>RNA polymerase begins RNA production near promoters after initiation factors or bacterial sigma-factor mechanisms help identify appropriate DNA sequences. Promoter accessibility, sequence recognition and regulatory complexes jointly influence the frequency of initiation. An occupancy model can illustrate how binding changes with free regulator concentration, but binding alone need not yield productive polymerase initiation. Direction is determined by the template and promoter configuration; distinct genes may be transcribed from opposite genomic strands.</p>\n<h3>Stepwise reasoning from molecular events to an observation</h3>\n<p>A useful molecular explanation begins by identifying the actual substrate and product in <strong>promoters and initiation</strong>, rather than merely naming a molecule associated with the phenomenon. The starting claim is that rNA polymerase begins RNA production near promoters after initiation factors or bacterial sigma-factor mechanisms help identify appropriate DNA sequences. In an active cell, production and consumption often occur simultaneously. For promoters and initiation, the distinction is especially consequential because direction is determined by the template and promoter configuration; distinct genes may be transcribed from opposite genomic strands.</p>\n<h3>Study design: choosing what to observe</h3>\n<p>Measure both factor binding and nascent transcription from engineered promoter variants. Alter one sequence motif while preserving genomic context as far as practical.</p>\n<p>For <strong>promoters and initiation</strong>, write down the expected results for three matched conditions: an unperturbed sample, a targeted perturbation of the proposed molecular step, and a rescue that restores function without otherwise changing the assay. A fourth condition should deliberately test an alternative explanation.</p>\n<h3>Quantitative thinking without false precision</h3>\n<p>For promoters and initiation, distinguishing the number of molecules from the proportion of molecules in a state is often essential.</p>\n<p>Start analysis with the limiting cases of an idealized model. When writing about promoters and initiation, state where idealization ends and empirical validation must begin.</p>\n<h3>Discriminating causes and ruling out artifacts</h3>\n<p>A strong occupancy signal cannot establish elongation competence, and promoter consensus scores are not universal quantitative predictors of expression. Build two alternative hypotheses that could mimic the predicted observation.</p>\n<p>A meaningful negative result needs equal scrutiny. Establish the dynamic range with positive and negative controls.</p>\n<h3>Specific molecular investigation</h3>\n<p>A repressor-binding assay shows a strong increase in occupancy when free ligand concentration rises from 6 to 18 nanomolar. Using a single-site dissociation constant of 6 nanomolar predicts a shift from half to three-quarter occupancy. A transcription reporter might nonetheless remain unchanged if the repressor binds a nonfunctional site or polymerase initiation is buffered. Place the same motif in two promoter contexts, measure binding independently, and quantify nascent RNA rather than relying only on mature steady-state signal. A nonbinding motif mutant can show whether the measured occupancy is sequence-specific. Different promoter architectures mean identical biochemical binding can produce distinct transcription outcomes.</p>\n",
          "secNumber": "4.1",
          "heading": "Promoters and initiation",
          "simulations": []
