          "secNumber": "4.9",
          "heading": "Guided molecular investigation",
          "content": "### Evidence integration lab\n\nThe aim of this studio is to compare molecular readouts that often have different denominators or experimental biases. Begin with a blinded sample label and a recorded protocol. In the analysis, separate substrate abundance, product abundance, reaction rate, and biological outcome. Use three genuinely independent biological samples per condition as an illustrative minimal planning exercise, while acknowledging that sample-size requirements depend on power, variation and experimental design. The hypothetical study below asks you to predict data patterns rather than pretending the described results have been observed.\n\n### Investigation 4.1: Promoters and initiation and RNA polymerase elongation\n\nA lab attempts to connect promoters and initiation to rna polymerase elongation using a shared perturbation. The first obligation is to keep the prediction narrow. RNA polymerase begins RNA production near promoters after initiation factors or bacterial sigma-factor mechanisms help identify appropriate DNA sequences. Promoter accessibility, sequence recognition and regulatory complexes jointly influence the frequency of initiation. An occupancy model can illustrate how binding changes with free regulator concentration, but binding alone need not yield productive polymerase initiation. Direction is determined by the template and promoter configuration; distinct genes may be transcribed from opposite genomic strands. The connected response should be measured separately because During elongation, polymerase traverses a DNA template and adds complementary ribonucleotides to a growing RNA chain. The enzyme can pause, backtrack and coordinate with RNA processing. Elongation rate varies with sequence, chromatin context and cellular conditions. In bacteria, coupled translation may affect transcription dynamics. Transcription-associated DNA supercoiling and its resolution influence both local progression and neighboring genomic processes. A single terminal measurement cannot determine whether the perturbation changed upstream material, a rate-limiting processing step, or sample survival. The study therefore predefines an early molecular observation and a late functional output, with an explicit normalization rule for each.\n\nThe proposed primary assay follows this plan: Measure both factor binding and nascent transcription from engineered promoter variants. Alter one sequence motif while preserving genomic context as far as practical. An orthogonal procedure addresses the downstream question: Pulse-label nascent RNA near the beginning and end of a long gene, then estimate traversal time under conditions controlling for initiation timing and RNA turnover. Its independent readout should not reuse the same molecular normalization error. Include a negative treatment, a validated positive comparison if available, and a material-balance or integrity control. When analyzing a gene perturbation, record allele or construct verification separately from RNA abundance and protein abundance. That prevents the convenient but unsupported inference that a verified DNA change automatically changed function.\n\nFor the interpretation, construct a table with rows for no change, increased upstream signal only, changed downstream signal only, and concordant changes in both. State at least two plausible mechanisms for each nonzero pattern. The most serious limitation may involve termination mechanisms: Avoid assuming all transcript ends originate at a programmed termination site; cleavage, degradation and library preparation also produce ends. A satisfactory report includes the type of observation that would falsify the chosen mechanism, the magnitude of technical uncertainty, and an explanation of which conclusions are about a model versus observed living cells. The outcome is a proposed experimental logic, not a claim that biological data were collected.",
          "simulations": []
