          "secNumber": "4.10",
          "heading": "Simulations and assumptions",
          "content": "The two simulation cards below are local, offline-capable numerical thought experiments. They are parameterized formula demonstrations, not mechanistic claims about a real cell type. Inspect the stated assumptions first, then vary an input, compare limiting cases and reset the model to its default.\n\n### Model focus: Single-site promoter occupancy\n\nA receptor or DNA-binding factor with free concentration L and one independent binding site with dissociation constant Kd has equilibrium occupancy \u03b8=L/(Kd+L). L and Kd must be measured in matching concentration units. At L=Kd the predicted occupancy is one-half; at L three times Kd it is three-quarters. The shape is saturating rather than linear. Affinity changes can shift the concentration response, yet transcription output may remain stable due to other promoter factors. The simulation reports percent occupancy, not polymerase activity or relative RNA production. A biological comparison should measure binding and nascent RNA separately, and a binding-defective motif can help test whether the factor acts at the proposed site.\n\n### Model application and sensitivity test\n\nChoose three free-regulator concentrations in the occupancy simulator: zero, one Kd and nine Kd. The predicted occupied fractions are zero, fifty and ninety percent. The model predicts smooth saturation because adding ever more regulator yields diminishing occupancy increases near the top of the binding curve. Suppose an RNA reporter changes abruptly over the same concentration interval. This discrepancy can suggest cooperative regulation, feedback, a threshold downstream of binding, or a separate factor that becomes limiting. A promoter without the regulator motif can test sequence dependence, while a factor-specific binding assay tests target engagement. Measure total protein and free nuclear concentration separately; the two quantities can differ due to sequestration. Avoid adjusting Kd merely to force agreement with an RNA output, because Kd describes a binding equilibrium and the reporter is the result of an entire biochemical pathway. Distinct model layers require distinct parameter measurements.\n\n### Model focus: RNA production and decay\n\nA deterministic RNA accumulation model combines constant synthesis s with first-order loss kM: dM/dt=s\u2212kM. Starting from M(0)=0, the solution is M(t)=(s/k)[1\u2212exp(\u2212kt)]. The simulation fixes s at twenty-five RNA molecules per hour. For k=0.5 h\u207b\u00b9 and t=4 h, the mean prediction is about 43.2 molecules, approaching a steady-state mean of fifty molecules. Lower k raises the steady-state abundance but usually lengthens the approach to that new level. Single-cell measurements may be broadly distributed when transcription occurs in bursts, so this smooth mean curve cannot stand in for a cell-level time trace. Test it with a time-course that measures nascent RNA and mature RNA, and inspect whether synthesis really remains constant after induction.\n\n### Model application and sensitivity test\n\nInvestigate the transient and steady-state effects of changing RNA loss rate while keeping synthesis fixed at twenty-five molecules per hour. At k=0.5 per hour the steady-state mean is fifty; at k=0.25 per hour it rises to one hundred, but the approach becomes slower. At one hour after induction, neither condition has reached its ultimate plateau. Record RNA counts at several early and late times and compare the models based on the entire time course, not one endpoint. The derivative at the very beginning is approximately the synthesis rate when M starts at zero, independent of k, which provides a useful limiting check. If transcription starts in bursts or one subpopulation remains silent, a single-cell dataset may have a variance much greater than this deterministic model captures. Extend the experiment by measuring nascent transcription separately from mature RNA and applying a pulse-chase strategy to test the removal constant. Different stages must not be conflated.",
