          "secNumber": "5.7",
          "heading": "Unit synthesis",
          "content": "A transcript is better understood as a time-dependent population of molecular isoforms than as a single static text string. Splicing can choose exon combinations, editing can modify selected nucleotides, and surveillance or other turnover mechanisms determine which RNA species persist long enough for detection. Alternative polyadenylation can alter regulatory regions without changing an encoded protein. Consider a locus producing two isoforms: one might dominate RNA sequencing reads and the other produce a more stable protein. A total-gene count would not convey the functional mixture. Design a measurement panel for pre-mRNA abundance, junction usage, RNA half-lives, ribosome association and final protein abundance. Every panel component should have a distinct interpretation and one important measurement failure mode. This design prepares readers to critique simplistic associations between a single RNA read count and a biological phenotype.",
          "simulations": []
