          "secNumber": "7.5",
          "heading": "Unit overview",
          "content": "Regulatory genes do not act in isolation. DNA packaging changes the accessibility of regulatory sequence; RNA and protein products can feed back on their own production; environmental inputs alter the operating point of a network. The same circuit may show different transient and steady-state behavior. The unit uses small mathematical response curves as hypotheses and emphasizes the experiments needed to distinguish regulation at DNA accessibility, transcription initiation, RNA processing or translation. Readers should separate a persistent expression state from proof of multigenerational epigenetic inheritance.",
