          "secNumber": "7.8",
          "heading": "Unit-specific integrated assessment",
          "content": "For gene regulation, compare two abstract circuits that reach the same equilibrium reporter value but respond differently to a short stimulus. One contains negative feedback and returns toward baseline; the other has a delayed response without feedback. Learners should sketch trajectories rather than infer dynamics from steady-state endpoints. Next ask how altered chromatin accessibility could modify the input to both circuits and why an accessibility signal would not prove a causal enhancer. Add a population measurement containing two cell states to test whether an average may shift through changes in state proportions alone. The assessment ends by defining a minimal study for persistent regulatory memory across cell divisions, specifying what must be tracked and how to exclude a DNA-sequence change. It is a test of causal interpretation across time, not memorization of epigenetic terminology.",
          "simulations": []
